/S /H1
/T (2.2 Comments)
/E (2.2 Comments)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Comments are notes you add within your code to explain what the code does. They start with the `#` symbol and are ignored by the Python interpreter when your code runs. )
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Comments help you and others understand the purpose of different parts of your code without affecting how the program works.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (They're like little reminders or explanations you leave for yourself or anyone reading your code.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Types of Comments)
/E (Types of Comments)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (There are two types of comments in Python, which are explained below:)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (1. Single-line comments: These comments start with # and extend to the end of the line. They are used for short explanations or notes on a single line of code.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
